  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2014-p8.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Note that all of these answer choices are of the form \\[ \\dfrac{n!(n + 1)!}{2} = \\dfrac{(n!)^2(n + 1)}{2}. \\] We have that \\((n!)^2\\) is square, so we need \\(\\dfrac{n + 1}{2}\\) to be square as well.\n\nThis means that \\(n + 1\\) must be twice a perfect square. The only choice we have is \\(n + 1 = 18,\\) which gives us \\(n = 17.\\)\n\nThus, D is the correct answer.",
      "hints": [
        "Write each choice as \\(\\frac{n!(n+1)!}{2}\\)",
        "Only \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) needs to be a square"
      ],
